class Solution {

    /*
    PROBLEM: MAXIMUM POINTS YOU CAN OBTAIN FROM CARDS
    =================================================

    APPROACH: FIXED SIZE SLIDING WINDOW


    PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
    ======================

    We are given an integer array arr and an integer pick.

    We have to select exactly 'pick' elements from either:
    - The beginning (left side) of the array.
    - The end (right side) of the array.

    We need to maximize the sum of the selected elements.

    Example:

    arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 1]
    pick = 3

    We have to select exactly 3 elements.

    Some possible selections:

    Left 3 elements:  [1, 2, 3] -> Sum = 6

    Right 3 elements: [5, 6, 1] -> Sum = 12

    2 from left and 1 from right: [1, 2, 1] -> Sum = 4

    Maximum possible answer = 12


    MAIN IDEA:
    ==========

    Instead of directly selecting elements from both ends,
    we use a different approach.

    We know that:

    Maximum Selected Sum = Total Sum - Minimum Unselected Sum

    Suppose:
    - Total number of elements = n
    - Number of elements to select = pick

    Then:

    Number of elements NOT selected = n - pick

    These unselected elements will always form one contiguous
    subarray in the middle of the array.

    Therefore, our task becomes:

    Find the minimum sum of a contiguous subarray
    having exactly (n - pick) elements.

    We can solve this using a FIXED SIZE SLIDING WINDOW.


    WHY DOES THIS WORK?
    ===================

    We can select elements only from the beginning or the end.

    This means we cannot leave some random elements unselected.

    All the elements that we do not select must be together
    in one continuous block.

    For example:

    arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 1]
    pick = 3

    n = 7
    k = n - pick = 4

    Suppose we leave these 4 elements unselected:

    [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 1]
        |-------|
        Unselected

    We select:
    - 1 from the beginning.
    - 2 from the end.

    Selected sum = 1 + 6 + 1 = 8

    Instead of checking every possible selection,
    we find the minimum sum subarray of size 4.


    SLIDING WINDOW VARIABLES:
    ========================

    i:
    Starting index of the current window.

    j:
    Ending index of the current window.

    k:
    Required window size = n - pick.

    window_sum:
    Stores the sum of elements in the current window.

    mini:
    Stores the minimum window sum found so far.

    total:
    Stores the sum of all elements of the array.


    ALGORITHM:
    ==========

    STEP 1:
    Calculate the total sum of the array.

    STEP 2:
    Calculate k = n - pick.

    This represents the number of elements that we
    are NOT going to select.

    STEP 3:
    If k == 0, it means pick == n.

    We have to select all elements, so return total.

    STEP 4:
    Initialize two pointers i and j at index 0.

    STEP 5:
    Traverse the array using pointer j.

    Add arr[j] to window_sum because this element
    is entering the current window.

    STEP 6:
    Check whether the current window size becomes k.

    Window size = j - i + 1

    If the window size becomes k:

    - Update mini with the minimum of mini and window_sum.
    - Store arr[i] in left.
    - Remove arr[i] from window_sum.
    - Move i forward.

    STEP 7:
    Move j forward and continue processing the array.

    STEP 8:
    After checking all windows, we have the minimum
    sum subarray of size k.

    STEP 9:
    Calculate the final answer:

    Answer = total - mini

    Return the answer.


    TIME COMPLEXITY:
    ================

    O(n)

    - First loop calculates the total sum: O(n).
    - Sliding window traverses the array: O(n).

    Overall: O(n) + O(n) = O(n).


    AUXILIARY SPACE:
    ================

    O(1)

    We use only a few variables.

    No extra array or data structure is required.
    */


    public int maxScore(int[] arr, int pick) {

        int n = arr.length; // Total number of elements in the array.

        // Number of elements that we do NOT need to select.
        // These elements will form one continuous subarray.
        int k = n - pick;

        int i = 0; // Starting index of the sliding window.
        int j = 0; // Ending index of the sliding window.

        int mini = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        // Stores the minimum sum of any window of size k.

        int window_sum = 0;
        // Stores the sum of elements in the current window.

        int total = 0;
        // Stores the sum of all elements in the array.


        // Calculate the total sum of the array.
        for(int a = 0; a < n; a++) {

            // Add the current element to the total sum.
            total += arr[a];
        }


        // If k == 0, it means pick == n.
        // We have to select all elements of the array.
        // Therefore, the answer is simply the total sum.
        if(k == 0) {

            return total;
        }


        // Traverse the array using pointer j.
        while(j < n) {

            // Current element entering the sliding window.
            int ele = arr[j];

            // Add the current element to the window sum
            // because arr[j] is now part of the window.
            window_sum += ele;


            // Check whether the current window size has become k.
            //
            // Window size = j - i + 1
            //
            // If it becomes k, we have found one complete
            // subarray of the required size.
            if(j - i + 1 == k) {

                // Update mini with the minimum sum found so far.
                //
                // If the current window sum is smaller than mini,
                // update mini.
                //
                // Otherwise, keep the previous minimum.
                mini = Math.min(mini, window_sum);


                // Store the leftmost element before removing it.
                int left = arr[i];

                // Remove the leftmost element from window_sum.
                //
                // We remove it because we want to move the
                // window forward and consider the next subarray.
                window_sum -= left;


                // Move the starting pointer forward.
                // This removes the leftmost element from the window.
                i++;
            }


            // Move the ending pointer forward.
            // The next element will enter the window.
            j++;
        }


        // We have now found the minimum sum subarray of size k.
        //
        // Maximum Selected Sum =
        // Total Sum - Minimum Unselected Sum
        //
        // Therefore, subtract mini from total and return the answer.
        return total - mini;
    }
}
